# M2 gate corpus - determinate-fragment claims (draft 1, 2026-11-21; amended 2026-11-22).
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS.  The corpus shadow of the four M2 gate rulings recorded in
# docs/PLAN.md sec. 8 decision 8 (accepted pending Verse-team revision): every case
# carries a `det=` classification computed from the decision-8 rulings *before* any
# engine ran, plus the normalisation pin the M2 machinery (T4 `det_check`, T5
# out-of-order lemma, T6 straight-line lowering, T7 no-switch theorem) must agree
# with.  Groups map to the rulings: A/C/D exercise the determinate-fragment claims,
# E lists the rejections `det_check` owes, F pins the DEV-20 materialisation
# obligations, G queues the flex/even-odd probes PLAN sec. 11 records.
#
# DERIVATION (ritual per PLAN sec. 8 decision 6).  Expectations derived by hand on
# 2026-11-21 from the rule text (docs/VERSE-RULES.md sec. 2-5, paper Figs. 8-13),
# the pinned oracle (Core/Expr.hs, Red.hs - already pinned by m0.texpr where cited),
# and kernel header comments (coq/Syntax.v prim_total; coq/Rewriting.v promote_ok /
# promoteHpGo).  No expectation was taken from evaluator output.  Amendment history
# (2026-11-22, first gate run): 27 of 29 pins executed exactly as derived - including
# the Group G promote-freshness refusals (all four `no row`) - and the two Group D
# block pins (oo_indep_blocks_fwd/rev) proved derivation errors: draft 1 assumed
# Subst reaches the tuple-argument position, but the Fig. 8 `S` grammar recorded in
# coq/Syntax.v (l.116-131) and the deferred Subst-Deep Tier-2 row (coq/Rewriting.v
# l.480-483) say it does not.  The pins are amended to the rule-text reading; the
# full re-derivation is in the two `why` fields and tools/corpus/README.md sec. 8.
#
# Classification vocabulary in the `why` fields:
#   det=yes        closed blocks only, total value candidate, no choice, no stuck shape
#   det=no(FAIL)   candidate exists but is `fail` - no value candidate (T4's `sem e = ret ...`)
#   det=no(stuck)  kernel selects no row: the Div-0 / open-block class (decision 8b rejects)
#   det=probe      a reading the corpus exists to expose, not to claim
#
# Grammar identical to tests/corpus/m0.texpr (tools/corpus/README.md sec. 1); the
# `det=` vocabulary lives inside `why` strings so both readers parse this unchanged.

defaults fuel 24

# ---------- group A: div/sub/mul sign matrix - the T6 division ladder ---------
# Line 452 (Fig. 10) is silent on sign.  Kernel and pinned oracle agree on FLOOR
# (Z.div / Haskell `div`, Red.hs:1346-1347; m0.texpr prim_div_negative_floor pins
# `(-7)/2 = -4`).  C truncation differs exactly where the pins marked [LADDER] -
# Lower.v must reproduce the *floor* value (decision 8b; DEV-20 `divZ` precedent).
# [SAME] marks sign combinations where truncation would agree - the ladder rungs
# that must be proven no-ops, pinned so their no-op status is measured, not assumed.

case arith_div_pos_pos
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdiv)) (tup (ret 7) (ret 2)))
  norm (ret 3)
  why "det=yes. 7/2 = 3: floor and truncation agree [SAME]. The rest of the matrix pins where they do not. Cite: line 452; prim_total Z.div (Syntax.v); T6 division table per decision 8b."

case arith_div_pos_neg
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdiv)) (tup (ret 7) (ret -2)))
  norm (ret -4)
  why "det=yes. [LADDER] 7/(-2): FLOOR -4; C truncation would give -3. This is the pair the Lower.v divZ ladder must reproduce (PLAN sec. 3 T3 half: C++ rounding recorded open at DEV-5; M2 ends at Clight, where CompCert's `/` truncates - the correction is emitted code, never a claim of agreement). Cite: Red.hs:1346-1347 (Haskell div floors), Syntax.v prim_total."

case arith_div_neg_neg_halves
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdiv)) (tup (ret -7) (ret -2)))
  norm (ret 3)
  why "det=yes. [SAME] (-7)/(-2): floor and truncation both give 3 (both-negative exact-ish half: truncation agrees whenever the true quotient rounds the same way). Pinned to measure, not assumed, that the ladder is silent here."

case arith_div_neg_pos
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdiv)) (tup (ret -6) (ret 4)))
  norm (ret -2)
  why "det=yes. [LADDER] (-6)/4 = -2 floor; C truncation -1. Second rungs-pair with arith_div_pos_neg."

case arith_div_neg_neg_quarter
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdiv)) (tup (ret -6) (ret -4)))
  norm (ret 1)
  why "det=yes. [SAME] (-6)/(-4): floor 1, truncation 1."

case arith_div_exact_neg
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdiv)) (tup (ret -8) (ret -2)))
  norm (ret 4)
  why "det=yes. [SAME] exact negative division: 4 either way. Rung-zero of the ladder."

case arith_sub_neg_neg
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv psub)) (tup (ret -5) (ret -3)))
  norm (ret -2)
  why "det=yes. Sub is total and sign-clean (line 452): (-5)-(-3) = -2, Z and C agree. Pinned so the ladder claim covers add/sub/mul as the measured no-op half."

case arith_mul_neg_pos
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pmul)) (tup (ret 7) (ret -4)))
  norm (ret -28)
  why "det=yes. 7*(-4) = -28, Z and C agree (multiplication has no rounding rung)."

case arith_ppls_neg_identity
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv ppls)) (ret -9))
  norm (ret -9)
  why "det=yes. pls identity on integers, negative included (m0.texpr prim_ppls_identity extends to -9 here)."

# ---------- group C: unify taxonomy and the Norm4 revisit probe ---------------

case unify_seq_left_drop_eqk1
  head r_seq
  expr (seq (ret 1) (unify (ret 4) (ret 4)))
  norm (ret 4)
  why "det=yes. R_Seq (line 483) drops the dead prefix; head steps are re-visited (tools/corpus/README.md sec. 3), so `4 = 4` then closes via EqK1 (line 432)."

case unify_seq_left_drop_eqk2
  head r_seq
  expr (seq (ret 1) (unify (ret 4) (ret 5)))
  norm fail
  why "det=no(FAIL). Same drop, then EqK2 (line 434): unequal literals fail. The candidate is fail, so T4's `sem e = ret (v, s')` has no witness: det_check owes this as a rejection (decision 8b's 'obvious stuck shapes' generalised: a *guaranteed* fail is as un-lowerable as a guaranteed Div-0)."

case norm4_revisit_partial
  head r_norm4
  expr (unify (unify (ret 6) (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret 2) (ret 3)))) (ret 6))
  norm (seq (unify (ret 6) (ret 5)) (ret 6))
  why "det=probe. Norm4 (VERSE-RULES sec. 3.3 table; Fig. 12 lines 479-491; the paper note: Norm4 exists for progress) fires at the root; the re-visited root has no row (neither side a value), so the structural rebuild leaves the partial shape - the README sec. 3.2 one-pass artifact. Probe content: a FAIR run collapses the left child (app -> 5, then EqK2 -> fail): the fair candidate is fail (det=no(FAIL) semantically), while the one-pass normalizer never performs the collapse. T4 must classify the fair semantics, T8 must prove the evaluator reaches it; this pin is the artifact both owe."

# ---------- group D: out-of-order and independence - the T5 pins --------------
# Decision 8a: determinate fragments are closed blocks only. Decision 8b/d: the
# lowering never sees an open block. The T5 claim itself (Wbisim of permuted
# fragments) lives in T5; what the corpus pins is the *measurable half*: permuted
# starts normalize, every candidate is a singleton, and the final component is the
# same value in every permutation.

case oo_left_assoc
  head r_norm5
  expr (seq (seq (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret 2) (ret 3))) (app (ret (primv pmul)) (tup (ret 4) (ret 5)))) (app (ret (primv psub)) (tup (ret 9) (ret 1))))
  norm (seq (ret 5) (seq (ret 20) (ret 8)))
  cands (seq (ret 5) (seq (ret 20) (ret 8)))
  cands_why "one candidate: cand_bag does not interchange binds (m0.texpr cand_bag law) - the bag holds the whole node"
  why "det=yes. Norm5 de-clutters `(e1;e2);e3` first; rebuild normalizes the children and the new root is not re-visited (README sec. 3.2), so the partial right-associated shape is the normal form. Prefix order is visible in the pin, not in the candidate count."

case oo_assoc_right
  head none
  expr (seq (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret 2) (ret 3))) (seq (app (ret (primv pmul)) (tup (ret 4) (ret 5))) (app (ret (primv psub)) (tup (ret 9) (ret 1)))))
  norm (seq (ret 5) (seq (ret 20) (ret 8)))
  cands (seq (ret 5) (seq (ret 20) (ret 8)))
  cands_why "same bag as oo_left_assoc, reached from a different starting shape"
  why "det=yes. Structurally different start (right-associated already), NO root row (left is not a value), rebuild only - and the normal form is identical to oo_left_assoc's pin. Pinning the *same* norm from distinct expressions is the corpus-side shadow of T5's fold/reassociate lemma."

case oo_swap_prefix
  head r_norm5
  expr (seq (seq (app (ret (primv pmul)) (tup (ret 4) (ret 5))) (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret 2) (ret 3)))) (app (ret (primv psub)) (tup (ret 9) (ret 1))))
  norm (seq (ret 20) (seq (ret 5) (ret 8)))
  cands (seq (ret 20) (seq (ret 5) (ret 8)))
  cands_why "one candidate, final leaf 8 - as in oo_left_assoc/oo_assoc_right"
  why "det=yes. Prefix swapped (mul before add): different normal form (prefix order is observable), same candidate count and same final component 8. T5's Wbisim is the claim that these three pins are *semantically* equal; the corpus pins what evaluation shows without claiming the theorem."

case oo_indep_blocks_fwd
  head none
  expr (seq (block ((tint 5)) (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret (var 0)) (ret 1)))) (block ((tint 7) (tint 9)) (ret (var 0))))
  norm (seq (block ((tint 5)) (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret (var 0)) (ret 1)))) (block ((tint 7) (tint 9)) (ret 9)))
  cands (seq (block ((tint 5)) (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret (var 0)) (ret 1)))) (block ((tint 7) (tint 9)) (ret 9)))
  cands_why "one candidate: the normal form itself (cand_bag law) - the seq holds it; block 1 is a stuck leaf (no row at a block head with an app body), block 2 resolved"
  why "det=yes (amended 2026-11-22). Draft 1 pinned the first block as collapsing to (ret 6); the first gate run flagged it (FAIL in both corners, driver log lines 17-21) and the re-derivation from rule text - not from the engine - says the draft was wrong: at a block head only Fig. 8 rows apply, RewSubst (coq/Rewriting.v l.398-403) matches only bodies of the shape fillS k (Ret (VVar i)) with k in the recorded S grammar (coq/Syntax.v l.116-131: hole, e=S, S=e, e;S, S;e, and the e[S]/S[e] variants), and the tuple-argument position inside an app body is not in that grammar - the value-context/block-inside variants are the Subst-Deep Tier-2 row deferred to M3 (coq/Rewriting.v l.480-483). So under one-pass rebuild (README sec. 3.2) block 1 keeps its app body: no Subst (unreachable), Gc1 premise false (slot 0 used), Promote premise needs a unify shape, no R_Seq/R_Norm4 at the seq root (Block is not a value, line 483). Block 2 matches the Subst pattern with k = Hole and resolves to (ret 9) - that half of draft 1 stands. M0 precedents the amended pin matches: subst_then_eqfail (Subst does reach unify-argument S positions) and betal_body_is_slot_variable (host-position blocks never collapse in m0 pins). T4's fair-sem side still resolves block 1 to the value 6 (paper-side slot lookup in evaluation, or the deferred Subst row - the kernel has no Get row, README sec. 5), so decision 8a's fragment classification is unchanged; what changes is that the *evaluator* artifact is a partial shape, exactly like norm4_revisit_partial above. The T5/T6 story (slot-disjoint closed blocks, order-agnostic straight-line lowering) is carried by the mirrored rev pin and the first three Group D cases."

case oo_indep_blocks_rev
  head none
  expr (seq (block ((tint 7) (tint 9)) (ret (var 0))) (block ((tint 5)) (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret (var 0)) (ret 1)))))
  norm (seq (block ((tint 7) (tint 9)) (ret 9)) (block ((tint 5)) (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret (var 0)) (ret 1)))))
  cands (seq (block ((tint 7) (tint 9)) (ret 9)) (block ((tint 5)) (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret (var 0)) (ret 1)))))
  cands_why "one candidate; mirror image of the amended fwd pin"
  why "det=yes (amended 2026-11-22, same correction as oo_indep_blocks_fwd - see its why for the full re-derivation). The swap pair now pins: same partial shape mirrored (resolved block in prefix position, stuck app-body block in suffix position), identical candidate count, same final component 9/6 pattern. Draft 1's collapse error was symmetric; the amendment is symmetric too. As a pair the two pins now show T5 what it actually needs: permutation invariance of the *evaluator* normal form (each permutation reaches the mirrored partial shape as its own normal form) without assuming collapse that the M0 row set does not provide."

# ---------- group E: rejection witnesses - what det_check must say no to ------

case wtn_choice_dup_two_entries
  head none
  expr (choice (ret 3) (ret 1))
  norm (choice (ret 3) (ret 1))
  cands (ret 3) (ret 1)
  cands_why "two candidates in the evaluator's bag list (m0.texpr cand_bag precedent); br2 idempotence (T2) identifies the denotation's SET with {3}, but the evaluator's bag keeps both entries - the set/evaluation distinction DESIGN-NOTES sec. 9 records"
  why "det=no(stuck-class). Choice node: no rewrite row (Figs. 9-12), T4's rejection is syntactic and conservative - decision 8b. A duplicate choice (3|3) would be a singleton at the br2 level; the conservative checker never inspects candidates, so it rejects the node class regardless. Pinned here in m0's shape; the idempotence half is recorded in cands_why rather than claimed."

case wtn_fail_root_no_value
  head none
  expr fail
  norm fail
  why "det=no(FAIL). Fail root: T4's witness form `sem e = ret (v, s')` has no witness (fail is a candidate set containing failure, not a value). Cite m0.texpr eq rows; DEV-5 half 2's `Fail`-catchability note is why the checker class is syntactic: catch can rewrite a fail candidate into a value candidate only if a catch node exists, and M0's grammar has none."

case wtn_open_block_in_seq_stays
  head none
  expr (seq (block ((tany)) (ret (var 0))) (ret 1))
  norm (seq (block ((tany)) (ret (var 0))) (ret 1))
  cands (seq (block ((tany)) (ret (var 0))) (ret 1))
  cands_why "one candidate: the declared-only block is decision 3's SStuck leaf (m0.texpr cand_bag_declared_block_is_a_leaf reading), and the seq holds it"
  why "det=no(stuck-class). Decision 8a: open blocks are outside the determinate fragment, and this is the witness: the lone declared slot takes no Subst (premise needs a valued slot) and no promote row (premise needs a unification), so the left component never becomes a value and R_Seq never fires. R_Seq needs the LEFT to be a value (line 483), so the open block also blocks the seq - the rejection reason is structural, matching decision 8a's fragment rule."

case wtn_unify_value_fail_stuck
  head none
  expr (unify (ret 3) fail)
  norm (unify (ret 3) fail)
  why "det=no(stuck-class). `Fail` is not a value and EqFail's pair shape (m0.texpr eqfail_primhead_vs_int: head-vs-head different roots) does not cover a bare literal against Fail: no row fires - tools/corpus/README.md sec. 1's worked example is this exact term (stuck, not fail)."

case wtn_div0_after_seq_drop
  head r_seq
  expr (seq (ret 1) (app (ret (primv pdiv)) (tup (ret 9) (ret 0))))
  norm (app (ret (primv pdiv)) (tup (ret 9) (ret 0)))
  why "det=no(stuck-class). R_Seq drops the dead prefix (head r_seq, line 483), re-visit, and the Div-0 row is PStuck (m0.texpr prim_div_zero_is_stuck; Syntax.v prim_total `b =? 0` arm). The witness class decision 8b owes to T7: with the guaranteed-literal zero pinned at the lowering site, the Clight division site is safe *because det_check proved the zero shape absent*; this case is the shape that must be rejected."

# ---------- group F: materialisation - the DEV-20 span cases ------------------

case dotdot_zero_start
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdotdot)) (tup (ret 0) (ret 4)))
  norm (ret (tupv 0 1 2 3 4))
  why "det=yes. Inclusive both ends (line 459; oracle `i <- [l..h]`, Red.hs:1384 - already pinned at 1..3 by m0.texpr prim_dotdot_range). A 5-element materialisation: F1/F2 are the *span* cases decision 8c's verifier-side bound must cap before the canonicalizer consumes external IR (DESIGN-NOTES section 9, DEV-20 tail)."

case dotdot_negative_span
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdotdot)) (tup (ret -3) (ret 1)))
  norm (ret (tupv -3 -2 -1 0 1))
  why "det=probe (span materialisation with a negative start; m0.texpr never exercised lo < 0 with the fixed Z.leb guard). Expected by the same inclusive reading (line 459; oracle Haskell [-3..1]); Syntax.v seq_z is the kernel materialisation and its negative handling is *pinned here, not yet measured* - if a corner disagrees, the finding lands next to DEV-20 like the seq_z finding did."

case iter_ifor_absent
  head none
  expr (iter ifor (block ((tint)) (ret 1)) (ret 2))
  norm (iter ifor (block ((tint)) (ret 1)) (ret 2))
  why "Absence claim (m0.texpr already pins iall/iif deferral): every Fig. 13 row needs the CtxC context-hole machinery, the head table defers the whole Iter family (RULES-AUDIT sec. 5), and nothing in the M2 corpus may assume it fires. The slot list is left untouched."

# ---------- group G: the flex queue - VC even/odd on the kernel side ----------
# PLAN sec. 11 records VC's even/odd term (Ariola & Blom) as the queued
# out-of-corpus test for the flex obligations; decision 8a keeps open blocks out of
# M2. These probes pin the refusal shapes NOW, so the refusal survives whatever the
# flex layer becomes. Cited rule: promote (line 416) with the freshness side
# condition fvsol(v) # dom(hp) union {x} (lines 416, 253 note); VC's cyclic-fill
# pathology and its u-occurs rule are the paper-side context.

case probe_forwardref_tuple_fill
  head none
  expr (block ((tany) (tany)) (unify (ret (var 1)) (ret (tupv 1 (var 0)))))
  norm (block ((tany) (tany)) (unify (ret (var 1)) (ret (tupv 1 (var 0)))))
  why "det=probe, kernel reading pinned; DIVERGENCE SHAPED TO SHOW. Body index 1 is slot 0 (index i names slot n-1-i), so this is VC's even/odd FIRST limb verbatim: `x = (1, y)` with x = slot 0, y = slot 1. Kernel: promote_ok (Rewriting.v ll. 224-226) counts mentions of ANY slot of the block, so the forward reference to the unfilled slot 1 blocks the row - head none. Paper (line 416 as VERSE-RULES sec. 3.2 records it): freshness is `fvsol(v) # dom(hp) union {x}`; slot 1 is declared, not in dom(hp), and is not x, so the VC row would FIRE and plant the forward reference - and its mutual composition with the second limb is the even/odd pair itself. Subst needs a valued slot (none here); the Eq pair rejects VVar-vs-tuple, so both readings agree on head none for the OTHER arms and disagree only on promote. together with probe_selfref_tuple_direct (x = (1, x), where BOTH readings refuse - a mention of x itself blocks under either reading), this quartet maps exactly where Timbda's kernel is stricter than the VC freshness condition: the even/odd pair cannot be built here because the kernel denies its first limb. M3's flex/recursive rows owe a ruling, with receipts, on which freshness the kernel keeps; until then this pin is the regression that any flex layer must confront openly, not silently. (Also recorded: m0.texpr promote_blocked_by_another_slot cites promote_ok with the paper's line-416 condition as if the two coincide - probe-grade evidence that they do not coincide for declared-slot references. Candidate spec-record finding; the ruling belongs to M3/T1.)"

case probe_forwardref_lam_fill
  head none
  expr (block ((tany) (tany)) (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret (lam tany (ret (var 2))))))
  norm (block ((tany) (tany)) (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret (lam tany (ret (var 2))))))
  why "det=probe, kernel reading pinned; DIVERGENCE SHAPED TO SHOW. VC's even/odd SECOND limb verbatim: y = (z.x), with y = slot 1 (body index 0 names slot n-1-0 = 1): crossing the lambda binder shifts the occurrence search by 1 (Syntax.v occurs arithmetic), so the lam's raw index 2 names slot 0 - the lam mentions x's NEIGHBOUR, not the binder being bound. Kernel: promote_ok's walk (Rewriting.v ll. 224-226) catches the slot 0 mention - head none. Paper: fvsol(v) = {slot 0}; the freshness set for binding slot 1 is dom(hp) union {slot 1} - disjoint - so the VC row would FIRE. Composed with probe_forwardref_tuple_fill (first limb), this is the even/odd pair VC builds through two individually-legal promotions; the kernel denies both limbs at the gate."

case probe_selfref_tuple_direct
  head none
  expr (block ((tany) (tany)) (unify (ret (var 1)) (ret (tupv 1 (var 1)))))
  norm (block ((tany) (tany)) (unify (ret (var 1)) (ret (tupv 1 (var 1)))))
  why "det=probe, refusal under BOTH readings. x = (1, x), x = slot 0 (body index 1): the mention is x itself, so the line-416 condition blocks the row under the paper reading exactly as promote_ok blocks it under the kernel's. Pinned as the agreement case of this group: the kernel/paper divergence is specifically about forward references to *other* unfilled slots, not about seeing self-mentions - a green run here is what keeps the divergence probes honest."

case probe_overfresh_declared_slot
  head none
  expr (block ((tany) (tany)) (unify (ret (var 1)) (ret (lam tany (ret (var 1))))))
  norm (block ((tany) (tany)) (unify (ret (var 1)) (ret (lam tany (ret (var 1))))))
  why "det=probe, DIVERGENCE SHAPED TO SHOW. The lam's raw index 1 escapes its binder to body level: it names slot 1, a DECLARED slot that is not x and not in dom(hp). Kernel: promote_ok (Rewriting.v ll. 224-226) counts mentions of ANY slot of the block, so the row is blocked - head none (kernel reading pinned). Paper (line 416): freshness is `fvsol(v) # dom(hp) union {x}`; a forward reference to a declared-but-unfilled slot satisfies it, so the VC row would FIRE and create exactly the forward-reference fill whose mutual version becomes the even/odd pair - the door VC's u-occurs rule and Ariola & Blom skew confluence stand at (PLAN sec. 11). Timbda's kernel already stands on the stricter side of the fork for free; M2's determinate fragment (decision 8a) keeps it dormant; M3's flex/recursive rows owe the ruling - with receipts - whether the stricter reading is kept or the VC freshness is matched. This case is the measurement: if a corner disagrees with the kernel reading pinned here, the disagreement is the finding."
